<h2>How game day tickets are priced on a resale marketplace</h2>
<p>Seat Outlet is a resale marketplace, so every set of game day tickets is priced by the seller. A listing may be above face value for a rivalry game or a playoff run, and below face value for a midweek game between teams with less demand. Compare a few listings for the same game rather than assuming one price tells the whole story.</p>
<p>For a step-by-step look at the checkout process, read <a href="/how-to-buy-tickets-online">how to buy tickets online</a>.</p>

<h2>Picking seats with game day tickets, sport by sport</h2>
<p>Every sport has its own sightlines, so the best game day tickets depend on what you want from the day. Think about whether you care most about the view, the atmosphere, the shade or the budget.</p>
<h3>Football game day tickets: NFL and college</h3>
<p>Sideline seats between the goal lines give you the broadest view of plays developing. End zone seats are often more affordable and are great for goal-line action. In outdoor stadiums, check which side gets afternoon sun or shade, and consider the weather for late-season games.</p>
<h3>Basketball and hockey game day tickets</h3>
<p>These games are played in compact arenas, so even upper rows keep you close to the game. Courtside and glass seats cost the most. Lower-bowl seats near center court or center ice offer a strong balance of view and price.</p>
<h3>Baseball</h3>
<p>Seats behind home plate give a close view of every pitch, while seats down the baselines are good for watching fielding and are often easier to find in groups. Many ballparks have covered sections, which helps for day games in summer.</p>
<h3>Soccer</h3>
<p>Midfield seats near the halfway line show the shape of the whole match. Seats behind the goals give you the chants and noise of supporters. Check each listing to see which end is the home supporters' section.</p>

<h2>What changes the price of game day tickets</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The opponent.</strong> Rivalries and top teams in the standings draw more demand.</li>
  <li><strong>The stakes.</strong> Playoff, tournament and season-opener games often cost more.</li>
  <li><strong>The day and time.</strong> Weekend and prime-time games are usually busier than weeknight games.</li>
  <li><strong>The seat location.</strong> Closer and more central seats cost more than corner or upper-deck seats.</li>
  <li><strong>The team's current form.</strong> A winning streak or a star player can lift prices quickly.</li>
</ul>

<div class="so-callout">
  <p><strong>Tip:</strong> If your group just wants to be at the game, look at the first few rows of the upper level. They are often priced well below lower-bowl seats while still giving a full view of the field or court.</p>
</div>

<h2>When to buy game day tickets</h2>
<p>For popular matchups, buy game day tickets as soon as you see seats that fit your group. For games that are less in demand, prices can shift as the date gets closer, but waiting is a gamble and may leave you with fewer seats together. Check <a href="/ticket-deals">ticket deals</a> and our <a href="/tickets-promo-code">promo code page</a> for current offers before you pay.</p>
<p>Each league publishes its own schedule, which is useful to cross-check dates and start times. You can view the official <a href="https://www.nfl.com/schedules/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NFL schedule</a> to plan around a full season.</p>

